# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- StringIO includes Enumerable[String] ---
#
# CRuby's `ext/stringio/stringio.c` makes `StringIO` Enumerable:
#
#   rb_include_module(StringIO, rb_mEnumerable);
#
# and `StringIO#each` yields the stream's lines, so a `StringIO` is an
# `Enumerable[String]` exactly as rbs core declares `IO` to be. Upstream
# `ruby/rbs` declares `class StringIO` with no `include` on every release the
# gemspec supports, so `StringIO.new(s).detect { ... }`, `.map`,
# `.each_with_index` and the rest of Enumerable reported
# `call.undefined-method` on correct code, and a block-taking `.select` fell
# through to `Kernel#select` (the IO multiplexer) and reported
# `call.wrong-arity`.
#
# The mixin sits behind the class in the ancestry, so every method StringIO
# declares itself (`each`, `each_line`, `each_char`, `readlines`, ...) keeps
# upstream's signature. A duplicate include, should upstream add one, builds
# on both rbs lines.
#
# Unconditional — NOT in `RbsLoader::LIBRARY_SUPPLEMENT_CORE_OVERLAYS`,
# because every environment Rigor builds already declares `StringIO`: in rbs
# `core/string_io.rbs` before 3.7, and from 3.7 on because
# `RBS::EnvironmentLoader#load` adds the `stringio` library itself whenever
# it loads core ("For migrating stringio to stdlib"). Gating on `stringio`
# would drop the mixin on rbs < 3.7, which has no such library, and from any
# environment that does not name it. The premise is pinned in
# `spec/rigor/environment/bundled_rbs_definition_build_spec.rb`.
class StringIO
  include Enumerable[String]
end
